During blackout periods, directors, executives, and designated employees are prohibited from:
- Purchasing or selling Company Securities;
- Making gifts or charitable donations of Company Securities;
- Making elections, changes, investment decisions or re-allocations in any 401(k) plan, pension plan, deferred compensation plan, or other benefit or deferral plans that involve Company Securities;
- Effecting cashless exercises of options.

However, the existence of a Blackout Period does not prohibit the delivery of shares in payment of tax withholding obligations upon the vesting of restricted stock or transactions pursuant to duly adopted Rule 10b5-1 Trading Plans.

The blackout periods generally begin at the close of business on the 15th day of the last month of each fiscal quarter, or 30 days prior to the scheduled release of financial results for the quarter (whichever is earlier), and occur through the end of the trading day on which financial results are released to the public.

Additionally, even outside of a blackout period, covered persons must never trade in company securities when they are in possession of material nonpublic information (MNPI).

